the stain replace  the card with the remaining stains back  in the chamber reseal it  and then put it back in its storage  temperature so there was a brief  interruption  of the environment within  within the chambers but it was probably  reestablished within  four to six hours so at each of the time  points would extract total rna  reverse transcribe it into complementary  dna  using oligodt and random primers  and then quantify  the abundance of the five prime and  three prime ends  of these three transcripts using our  rqtcr assay and the question was  can we follow or can we notice any  effect  of temperature and our relative humidity  on rna degradation rate  so here are three of our  storage conditions in terms of  temperature  37 degrees 20 degrees and 4 degrees  and then the colored curves on  in each of these panels represent the  relative humidity and i've got them  listed here  in in trying to consider this data and  digest this data  one wants to try and have  a description of an environmental  condition  that's defined by a single mathematical  value  um so in thinking about  this problem uh what came to mind was  the use of a heat index  value and a heat index value if you  watch your weather  especially in the summertime weatherman  will often give you a combined  [Music]  value a value that combines both  temperature and relative humidity into a  single number  and so we're exploring that right now as  our approach to  uh consider combine both humidity  and temperature into a single single  entity  anyway take home lesson from from this  study is that  both heat and humidity contribute to an  accelerated rate  of rna degradation uh the most severe  and most rapid rate of rna degradation  occurs  at 37 degrees and 75 excuse me a minute  that's going to see 37 degrees and 75  relative humidity you can see that uh  the rate  of degradation of the five prime min of  the s100 812 message  um it increases very very quickly and  actually  levels off after only about four weeks  of storage  whereas at 20 degrees and 35  humidity the conditions um  sort of reflecting what we had in the  laboratory  you can the curve is still increasing  out to  six months of storage pretty much like  we saw uh  previously in in our studies with  with bloodstains initially so  the interesting thing here is that at  four degrees uh degradation almost halts  completely you can see a little bit of  increase  in the delta cq value at four degrees  and seventy-five percent related  humidity  uh but at four degrees and ten percent  relative humidity things are  uh virtually stable over an entire  six-month storage period  so for any of you in in the listening  world  that are studying rna  in dried evidentiary type samples  the take home lesson is that once you  collect your sample from a scene store  to four degrees  in a dry environment to preserve the  integrity  of those transcripts for long periods  so how do we factor all of this  environmental effect into an algorithm  that will allow us to continue to  estimate the age of the stain  well we chose to  have chosen we haven't gotten there yet  we've chosen to start  off by comparing degradation rate  constants and the degradation  rate constant in in this discussion is  basically  how long it's the maximum delta cq value  that is achieved  during uh storage all right  so when it gets to when the curve gets  to its highest value  the delta c value there minus the delta  cq value at time  0 divided by the amount of storage time  it takes to get to that maximal value  and so if we compare these rate  constants  if we control for temperature and vary  uh do comparisons or rate constants for  different humidities  or control for relative humidity and  assess the effects of different  temperatures  it's the take-home lesson from from all  of this work  is that um if you compare  at a at a single temperature 75  humidity versus 35 humidity you get  about a two to three-fold increase  in the degradation rate at the higher  value  likewise if you  have a single humidity and compare the  effective temperature  again it's about a two to three-fold  increase in the rate of degradation  of these transcripts  at a higher temperature so  we're still dealing with  evaluating this data to figure a way  to incorporate it into  as i said some sort of algorithm that  retains  the accuracy in estimating the age of a  staying  clearly you can get the environmental  conditions  from the national weather  organization  [Music]  the cycling up and down of temperature  and up and down of humidity  uh is another variable that we're going  to have to try and  and figure uh incorporate into our  algorithm  but um that that's where we're going  in the future now another interesting  fact is that once degradation starts  if you shift the sample from a high  temperature high humidity  sort of the blue line here in this curve  for the s 1812 transcript again  if you incubate it at 37 degrees and 75  humidity and then at some point shift  that environment to four degrees in a  dry  dry dry environment you can shut down  degradation  so it is reversible in the sense that  you can  promote degradation or [ __ ] or  stop degradation altogether just by  and it happens fairly quickly  [Music]  once you make once you make that  temperature shift to the more stable  environment  degradation halts which to me suggests  that it may be  a chemical process and is involved here  in degradation as opposed to enzymatic  so temperature and environment  another part of the environment is the  substrate that  a body fluid stain resides on or is  deposited on  so we wanted to assess the effect of the  substrate as well  and um substrates can include clothing  uh or surfaces metal ceramic wood  plastic  even carpet and so we created stains  on blood stains on all of these  different substrates  and then stored them for periods of up  to six months  in the laboratory again under  under dark conditions  the results of that study i'm just going  to show you a couple of  um a couple of surfaces which include  carpet  and denim and the take-home lesson from  all of our substrate studies  is that there really is no effect on the  rate of rna degradation  uh depending upon the surface when  compared against  blood stains created with spain carbs  that we're using sort of as our control  so the effect of  the substrate is not very significant  it really doesn't have much of an effect  on the rate of rna regulation which is  good  because that would be if it did have an  effect that would be a further  complicator or distractor  in trying to develop an algorithm with  which to estimate time  well the newest thing where we the  newest question we've asked  is if we can estimate what day  a stain was created on can we estimate  the time of day  a staying was created and  we thought that the way perhaps to  investigate this question was to exploit  rhythmic changes in gene expression that  are  associated with the circadian rhythm and  there's a tremendous amount of  literature  out there describing genes whose  expression seems to vary  over the daily cycle and so our approach  was to  create blood stains and to assess the  abundance  of messenger rnas transcribed from these  genes  over the 24-hour daily cycle  so the experiment was somehow we managed  to recruit a large number of volunteers  who are willing to prick their finger  create a blood stain  on a collection card every four hours  around the clock and we had  i want to say 20 to 30 males  of all different ages and about the same  number of females  who were uh participating who were  willing to volunteer for this study  so once we create a stain  at a select time during the day that  staying within  uh one to two days would be transferred  back to the laboratory  rna extracted from it cdna prepared  and then quantification of the  individual  transcripts originating from genes  associated with a circadian rhythm  would be quantified using ppcr in this  particular  set of experiments we developed qpcr  primers that targeted the middle  of each of these transcripts because  from all of our previous research  we knew that that was the most stable  part  of a messenger rna was the least  likely to be degraded during that two  hours of  storage or  that the time between the stain was  created in the timing  that if the rna was extracted so we got  all this data we analyzed it by sex  whether it's male or female  also by age and tenure increments from  uh 20 to 60 years of age  and interestingly here's some of the  data  that was produced and we had  most of the transcripts that we looked  at  yielded results that either were  unchanging  over a 24-hour period or  produce data very much akin to what you  see  in um the top right panel there for the  per  three transcript and nails huge error  bars  now it's important for you to know that  these error bars  do not reflect technical variability  in our qpcr assay rather  these error bars reflect biological  variability  among the 20 odd males that  were tested so  it's kind of interesting that there's  such widely varying  levels of gene expression four genes  that  are have been suggested to be associated  with the circadian rhythm  that in males for this particular gene  the rhythm is all over the place  however if you look at that same  transcript in females  you see fairly tight variability in the  time points and um the data  for uh cpu values from  noon to the evening on the right side of  that  that curve are significantly different  from the cpu values on the left side of  that panel  so it would be possible using this  marker in a female blood stain  to distinguish whether it was if that  blood stain was created in  the afternoon or in the evening  or early morning likewise another  transcript of tpoh which is  tyrosine metabolizing enzyme that  ultimately  ends up um involved in the production of  melatonin  which of course is is typically high in  in the evenings when you get sleepy  again  those are statistically significant  differences in cpu values  from the early evening 10 11 o'clock  versus the early morning  so we're not where we need to be yet  we're still looking at  uh additional transcripts that seem to  show  cyclic variation in uh abundance inside  blood stains  but we feel that at least proof of  concept here  if we have a whole lot of these markers  that we can  analyze in a test  that we may be able to  discriminate when during the day within  a couple of hours  a stain was created so that's where we  are  right now i want to acknowledge the  people in my laboratory that have been  contributed to the studies that i talked  about today  and to emphasize once again  that time is an important concept to the  forensic scientist  and um ultimately  we hope that from a single body fluid  stain recovered from a crime scene  we can determine who's it it we can  determine what it is whose it is and  when it was deposited  and all of those questions are going to  be very relevant to  our investigation of the crime so thanks  for your attention  i will take any questions i guess by  email after those of you in the audience  think about what i've said today and  i'll do my best to try and answer them  thank you very much
 

